    "fullSolution": "**Find arc length of $y = \\frac{2}{3}x^{3/2}$ from $x=0$ to $x=3$**\n\n**Formula:** $L = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{1 + (y')^2}\\, dx$\n\n**Step 1: Find derivative**\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x^{1/2} = \\sqrt{x}$$\n\n**Step 2: Set up integral**\n$$L = \\int_0^3 \\sqrt{1 + (\\sqrt{x})^2}\\, dx = \\int_0^3 \\sqrt{1+x}\\, dx$$\n\n**Step 3: Integrate**\n$$\\int (1+x)^{1/2}\\, dx = \\frac{2}{3}(1+x)^{3/2} + C$$\n\n**Step 4: Evaluate**\n$$L = \\left[\\frac{2}{3}(1+x)^{3/2}\\right]_0^3 = \\frac{2}{3}\\left(4^{3/2} - 1^{3/2}\\right) = \\frac{2}{3}(8-1) = \\frac{14}{3}$$\n\n**Answer:** $\\displaystyle\\frac{14}{3}$ units",
